(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_biotechnologyprinciplesandprocesses=[{id:"neet-2026-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Recombinant DNA technology",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Transformation \u2014 I. Restriction enzyme
B. Cloning site \u2014 II. Transfer DNA to host bacteria
C. Selection \u2014 III. Replication
D. Ori \u2014 IV. Antibiotic
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Transformation is transfer of DNA to the host bacteria (A-II).
Cloning site is a DNA segment in a plasmid vector with unique recognition sequences for restriction enzymes (B-I).
Antibiotics help in selection of recombinants (C-IV).
Ori is the DNA sequence where the host's replication machinery starts copying the plasmid (D-III).`},{id:"neet-2026-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"During PCR, primers bind to the DNA strands in the ______step.",o:["extension","annealing","ligation","denaturation"],c:1,s:`PCR has three steps: denaturation, annealing and extension.
Primers bind to the DNA strands in the annealing step.`},{id:"neet-2026-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors, plasmids",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Plasmids are autonomously replicating DNA.
Statement II : Plasmids are extrachromosomal DNA.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Plasmids are used as cloning vectors in genetic engineering.
Plasmids are autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA.
They can replicate inside bacterial cells independent of the control of the chromosomal DNA.`},{id:"neet-2026-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of genetic material",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: In recombinant DNA technology, lysozyme is used for disrupting bacterial cells while cellulase is for plant cells.
Reason R: Isolation of genetic material needs disruption of cells.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`DNA is enclosed within membranes, so the cell must be broken open to release it.
Other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids are released along with it.
This is done with enzymes: lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2025-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Polymerase chain reaction",q:"Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies DNA following the equation.",o:["N\xB2","2\u207F","2n + 1","2N\xB2"],c:1,s:`PCR amplifies DNA as per the equation 2\u207F, where n is the number of cycles.
Example: 3 PCR cycles give 2\xB3 = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 2 = 8 DNA fragments.`},{id:"neet-2025-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Selection of recombinants",q:"In the above represented plasmid, an alien piece of DNA is inserted at EcoRI site. Which of the following strategies will be chosen to select the recombinant colonies?",o:["Using ampicillin & tetracycline containing medium plate.","Blue color colonies will be selected.","White color colonies will be selected.","Blue color colonies grown on ampicillin plates can be selected."],c:2,s:`White-coloured colonies will be selected.
The alien DNA is inserted at the EcoRI site, so the \u03B2-galactosidase gene there undergoes insertional inactivation.
This gene gives blue-coloured colonies, but since it is inactivated, white colonies are produced.
The ampicillin and tetracycline resistance genes stay intact, so the DNA will show ampR and tetR.`,fig:{w:318,h:190,alt:"A circular plasmid with Tet R, BamH1, Ori, amp, EcoRI and beta Galactosidase marked around it.",d:[["p","M 36.6 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 213 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 36.6 91.2 Z"],["p","M 109.2 10.8 L 165 13.2 L 192 39 L 184.8 49.2 L 156 40.8 L 112.2 24 Z","f"],["l",114,13.2,118.8,25.2],["l",129,18,133,29.8],["l",144,22.8,147.1,34.3],["l",159,27.6,161.3,38.9],["l",174,32.4,175.4,43.4],["l",189,37.2,189.6,48],["p","M 112.8 19.2 Q 162 21 190.8 43.2","B"],["p","M 25.2 57 L 73.8 67.2 L 73.8 99 L 19.8 118.8 Z","f"],["l",31.2,61.2,28.2,111.9],["l",37.8,62.5,34.8,109.6],["l",44.4,63.8,41.4,107.3],["l",51,65.2,48,105],["l",57.6,66.5,54.6,102.7],["l",64.2,67.8,61.2,100.4],["l",70.8,69.1,67.8,98],["p","M 87 145.8 L 145.2 150 L 145.2 159.6 L 93 162.6 Z","f"],["l",93,147.4,93,160.8],["l",98.4,147.8,98.4,160.8],["l",103.8,148.2,103.8,160.8],["l",109.2,148.6,109.2,160.8],["l",114.6,148.9,114.6,160.8],["l",120,149.3,120,160.8],["l",125.4,149.7,125.4,160.8],["l",130.8,150.1,130.8,160.8],["l",136.2,150.4,136.2,160.8],["l",141.6,150.8,141.6,160.8],["p","M 205.2 73.2 Q 213 114 175.2 145.2"],["p","M 211.2 73.2 Q 218.4 117 182.4 150"],["p","M 208.2 78 Q 216 114 177.6 147.6","B"],["l",219,73.8,232.2,82.8],["l",232.2,82.8,190.8,157.2],["l",181.2,150,190.8,157.2],["l",186,103.2,226.8,103.2],["l",214.8,124.8,231,130.8],["l",69,25.8,69,42],["t",25.2,28.8,"BamH1","s"],["t",169.8,14.4,"Tet R","s"],["t",1.2,91.2,"Ori","s"],["t",229.2,108,"EcoRI","s"],["t",225,141.6,"\u03B2 Galactosidase","s"],["t",100.8,180,"amp","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Bioreactors",q:"Identify the part of a bio-reactor which is used as a foam braker from the given figure.",o:["A","B","D","C"],c:3,s:`Part labelled C is the foam breaker.
B is the motor, A is the flat bladed impeller, D is sterile air inlet.`,fig:{w:210,h:266.5,alt:"Stirred-tank bioreactor with parts A, B, C and D pointed out by arrows.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B",""],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C",""],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A",""],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D",""]]},sfig:{w:330,h:266.5,alt:"Bioreactor with B motor, C foam breaker, A flat bladed impeller and D sterile air inlet marked.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B - Motor","s"],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C - Foam breaker","s"],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A - Flat bladded impeller","s"],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D - Sterile air","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The DNA fragments extracted from gel electrophoresis can be used in construction of recombinant DNA.
Statement II : Smaller size DNA fragments are observed near anode while larger fragments are found near the wells in an agarose gel.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:0,s:`Cutting DNA with restriction endonucleases gives DNA fragments, which are separated by gel electrophoresis.
Separated DNA bands are cut out of the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is elution.
The purified fragments are used to build rDNA by joining them with cloning vectors.
In gel electrophoresis, fragments separate by size through the sieving effect of agarose.
The smaller the fragment, the farther it moves from cathode towards anode.`},{id:"neet-2025-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gene cloning enzymes",q:`Which of the following enzyme(s) are NOT essential for gene cloning?
A. Restriction enzymes
B. DNA ligase
C. DNA mutase
D. DNA recombinase
E. DNA polymerase
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C and D only","A and B only","D and E only","B and C only"],c:0,s:`Gene cloning is a process where a specific gene or DNA sequence is isolated and copied, making many identical copies.
In gene cloning, restriction enzymes, DNA ligase and DNA polymerase are mainly used.`},{id:"neet-2025-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Blue-white selection",q:`The blue and white selectable markers have been developed which differentiate recombinant colonies from non-recombinant colonies on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate.
Given below are two statements about this method:
Statement I : The blue coloured colonies have DNA insert in the plasmid and they are identified as recombinant colonies.
Statement II : The colonies without blue colour have DNA insert in the plasmid and are identified as recombinant colonies.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:3,s:`Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.
Recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the enzyme \u03B2-galactosidase.
This inactivates the gene for making this enzyme (insertional inactivation).
So colonies with an insert do not produce colour and are identified as recombinant.
Non-recombinant colonies make blue colour in the presence of the chromogenic substrate.`}]})();
